INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S KITCHEN - DUSK

Salt has eaten the window frames pale. Soup steams on a black stove, and four chipped cups wait on an oilcloth the color of old teeth.

ELOWEN PASCOE (30s, wool cardigan, a woman who has learned to move quietly) ladders bread onto a board. Across the table, her husband CALLUM PASCOE (30s, broad, sunburnt) peels an orange in one unbroken spiral, offering her the whole bright ribbon of it.

CALLUM
Thought you'd like it.

ELOWEN
You remembered.

She takes it. Smiles. The kettle sings. It is a good evening, the kind that asks nothing.

Boots on the stair. FINN ARDEN (20s, oilskin, rain in his hair) fills the doorway with his hat in his hands. Behind him, the youngest, MAIRI PASCOE (12, braids), clutches the banister.

FINN
Evening, Mrs. Pascoe.

Elowen drops the orange peel. Callum looks at her, then at him, then at her again, and all three of them stand quite still.

MAIRI
Mam? Why does Da have Finn's letters?

Callum opens his coat. A bundle of envelopes tied with blue thread. Elowen's handwriting on every one.

CALLUM
Sit down, both of you.

Neither sits. The soup begins, quietly, to boil over.
